# scripts/.private-tokens — host-local blocklist for check-host-agnostic.sh
#
# Copy this file to scripts/.private-tokens (which is gitignored and MUST NOT be
# committed) and add one private token per line. Each token is treated as a
# private machine hostname / identifier that must never appear in published
# content; the lint flags both its literal and lower-cased form.
#
# check-host-agnostic.sh ALSO auto-derives tokens from the basenames of your
# ~/.claude/hosts/*.md config files, so listing them here is only needed for
# tokens that have no corresponding host-config file.
#
# Blank lines and lines starting with '#' are ignored.
#
# Example (remove the leading '#', use your real private hostnames):
# my-private-laptop
# my-corp-host
#
# Tokens are matched as literal substrings anywhere in published content, so
# this file is also the place to block a private *repo slug* that must never be
# committed — e.g. the Obsidian vault the `vault-cloud` skill talks to, which is
# supplied at runtime via OBSIDIAN_VAULT_REPO precisely so its slug stays out of
# this repo. Add the OWNER-QUALIFIED form so the guard targets the real leak and
# nothing else:
# <owner>/<private-vault-repo>
#
# Do NOT list the bare repo name on its own (e.g. just the '<private-vault-repo>'
# half): it appears legitimately in generic prose and in the design doc's own
# filename references, so a bare token would flag those as false positives. The
# owner-qualified slug appears nowhere in the tree today, so it only fires on an
# actual reintroduction.
